<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">League of Lezhë</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a> was an alliance of Albanian noble families founded at Lezhë in March 1444 under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>, with Venetian-protected Lezhë as its meeting place. Members such as the Dukagjini and the Crnojević of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> pledged men and money for war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, though the alliance soon weakened through rivalries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe.jpg" alt="League of Lezhë" width="320" height="610" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:League_of_Lezh%C3%AB,_1448-1468.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ottoman advance into Albania met a number of separate Albanian principalities. The first battle against Ottoman forces there was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Battle of Savra</a> on 18 September 1385. After the Ottoman civil war of 1402-13, Ottoman forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Kruja</a> from the Thopia in 1415, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> from the Muzaka in 1417, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kaninë</a> in 1417, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a> in 1418. Some Albanian nobles revolted in 1432-36. In November 1443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> captured Kruja and declared its independence from the sultan.</p><p>The League was founded on 2 March 1444 at an assembly of lords that included Lekë Zaharia and his vassals Pal and Nicholas Dukagjini, Pjetër Spani, Lekë Dushmani, three members of the Balsha family, Andrea Thopia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjergj-arianiti">Gjergj Arianiti</a>, Theodor Corona Muzaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-crnojevic">Stefan Crnojević</a>. Delegates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> attended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> was proclaimed Chief of the League of the Albanian People and commanded a combined force of 8,000 warriors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Each lord kept his own domain and its internal affairs and contributed men and money to the alliance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> had no right to interfere in the domains of other nobles and acted only as supreme military leader, first among equals. The League was organised under Venetian patronage and was placed through treaties under King Alfonso V, with Skanderbeg as captain general. Barleti called the meeting a general council; the name League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a> was coined by later historians.</p><p>The League's forces won victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Torvioll in 1444, Mokra in 1445 and Otonetë in 1446, and lost at Svetigrad in 1448. The pro-Venetian Balsha and Crnojević left soon after the founding, which led to the Albanian-Venetian War of 1447-48. It ended on 4 October 1448 with a treaty signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> and Nicholas Dukagjini, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept its Albanian possessions and paid 1,400 ducats a year. The alliance was precarious, and by 1450 only a core under Skanderbeg and Arianiti was fighting; some Dukagjini made peace with the Ottomans.</p><p>Ottoman sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Kruja</a> began on 14 May 1450 and failed, as did later ones in 1466 and 1467. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> died in 1468 the League began to break up. Resistance collapsed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Shkodra in 1479.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Some historians regard the League as an independent Albanian state, while others hold that it was only a military league. It gave the first sign of Albanian unity under an Albanian leader. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> and the League have become part of Albanian historiography, and the period is seen by many as mythical and unchallengeable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “League of Lezhë” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the besieging Ottoman army defeated, Skanderbeg, leader of the <b>League of Lezhë</b>, entered his main fortress of Krujë, which had held out since June 1466.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Leading the <b>League of Lezhë</b>, Skanderbeg defeated an Ottoman army under Ali Pasha at Torvioll in Albania, attacking its flanks and rear with troops hidden in the woods.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>An Ottoman army led by Sultan Murad II and his crown prince Mehmed II besieged Krujë, the Albanian capital of Skanderbeg's <b>League of Lezhë</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-14">14 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
